  "query_preview": "..\n\nToday through Monday...\n\nAs the region transitions to southwest flow aloft, satellite is\nbeginning to show increased cloud cover across southern MT and\nnorthern WY. This disturbance is expected to bring isolated to\nscattered showers during the early morning hours with a few \nthunderstorms during the afternoon. With high based showers \nexpected, showers and thunderstorms will be capable of producing \ngusty winds today. Into Wednesday a slightly stronger disturbance \nmoves up from southwest MT and clips the region with a front.  \nPrefrontal breezy winds will be out of the west/southwest and are\nexpected to be in the 30s (mph) through the morning and early \nafternoon. As hot and dry conditions return, elevated isolated to \nscattered showers and thunderstorms Wednesday afternoon are \nexpected to bring gusty winds. Gusts in the 40-55 mph range will \nbe common with any stronger storms. Another note to add that with \nthese dry conditions, elevated fire weather conditions will be of \nconcern for those who have not recently received appreciable \nprecipitation. Similar breezy conditions are expected for areas \nwest of Billings following the front Thursday, thus continuing \nelevated fire weather conditions. There is still some uncertainty \namongst ensembles on how far west the ridge across the central \nplains reaches and if the region will get in on any of the \nmoisture from the low persisting over the Pacific NW coast. With \nthis, temperatures will remain seasonal today. For the remainder \nof the period, near normal temperatures are forecast across the \nfoothills west of Billings (upper 70s) with the remainder of the \nregion in the 80s to low 90s. TS",
